---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { NAME, EMAIL } from '../../consts';
import { url } from '../../lib/posts';

const REPO_URL = 'https://github.com/habibafaisal/falcon';
const METHOD_URL = `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/METHOD.md`;
const CASES_URL = `${REPO_URL}/tree/main/examples/case-studies`;

const VIDEO_ID = 'AUtNaEk-cLc';
const VIDEO_URL = `https://youtu.be/${VIDEO_ID}`;

const title = 'Falcon: a Claude Code harness for codebases where a silent bug is expensive';
const description =
  'Falcon is an open-source Claude Code harness for careful projects (money, health, auth, data, infra): risk-tiered ownership, tests seen failing, independent review, and every caught miss turned into a check.';

const appLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode',
  name: 'Falcon',
  description,
  codeRepository: REPO_URL,
  programmingLanguage: 'Python',
  license: 'https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT',
  url: new URL(url('/falcon/'), Astro.site).href,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: NAME, url: new URL(url('/'), Astro.site).href },
});

const videoLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'VideoObject',
  name: 'Claude Code Workflow That Catches Silent Bugs: AI Coding Agent Harness (Falcon)',
  description:
    'A walkthrough that follows one change, a fee split on a money platform, through every step of Falcon: install, planning, the codebase oracle, test-first build, review, mutation sweep, operator testing and the guard ladder.',
  thumbnailUrl: `https://i.ytimg.com/vi/${VIDEO_ID}/hqdefault.jpg`,
  uploadDate: '2026-10-27',
  duration: 'PT2M36S',
  contentUrl: VIDEO_URL,
  embedUrl: `https://www.youtube.com/embed/${VIDEO_ID}`,
});
---

<Base title={title} description={description} ogImage="/og/falcon.png">
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={appLd} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={videoLd} />
  </Fragment>

  <h1>Falcon</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A Claude Code harness for codebases where a <em>silent</em> bug is expensive: the kind that
    passes its own tests and is wrong anyway. Money, health, auth, data pipelines, infrastructure.
  </p>

  <p class="cta">
    <a class="button" href={REPO_URL}>Falcon on GitHub</a>
  </p>

  <div class="video">
    <iframe
      src={`https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/${VIDEO_ID}`}
      title="Falcon walkthrough"
      loading="lazy"
      allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
      referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
      allowfullscreen></iframe>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">One change, a fee split, followed through every step. <a href={VIDEO_URL}>Watch on YouTube</a>.</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Why I built it</h2>
    <p>
      Our tests passed, but the ledger was off from the bank. The agent had written the code and the
      tests in the same session, so the tests agreed with the code and both were wrong. A balanced
      ledger sums to zero either way, so no balance check could have told them apart.
    </p>
    <p>
      Test-first doesn’t help when the same mind writes the reading of the spec, the test and the
      code. Most agent harnesses spend the same process on every change. Falcon spends it where a
      silent bug would cost something.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Risk-tiered ownership.</strong> Work is tiered by what a silent bug would corrupt,
        not by how hard it looks. Boilerplate is delegated. Critical code is UNDERSTAND-IT: the
        human hears the design one concept at a time and ratifies a plan before any code is
        written.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Graded evidence, independent review.</strong> Nothing is just “done”. The builder
        never verifies its own work; a fresh agent does. A test only counts once it’s been seen
        failing with its guard broken.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Misses become checks.</strong> Every miss a human catches becomes an escape record
        and an enforced check in the same session, not a note.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A guard ladder.</strong> Rules that get broken move from prose, to failing checks, to
        hooks that block.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>State lives in the repo.</strong> Tiers, plans, the mistake log and the session
        handoff are files, so they survive <code>/clear</code> and get reviewed like code.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Results so far</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>20 escapes</strong>: misses a human caught after every automated layer passed. Each shipped a new check the same session.</li>
      <li><strong>27 mistake classes</strong> logged, each with the guard that stops it.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>2 recurrences</strong> after a guard existed, and both were the guard ladder at
        work: a prose rule that became a check, and a check widened from twin functions to twin
        entry points.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <a href={CASES_URL}>case studies</a> walk through seven of those misses and the check each
      one produced. <a href={METHOD_URL}>METHOD.md</a> explains the loops one at a time.
    </p>

    <h2>Install</h2>
    <p>Once per machine, clone Falcon and add the <code>falcon-init</code> skill:</p>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/habibafaisal/falcon.git ~/falcon
mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills && cp -r ~/falcon/skills/falcon-init ~/.claude/skills/</code></pre>
    <p>
      Then open Claude Code in your repo and say <strong>“set up falcon”</strong>. It copies the
      files in and runs a short interview where you choose what counts as UNDERSTAND-IT for your
      domain. Your choices are recorded as the project’s first decision record.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">MIT licensed. Falcon is not affiliated with Anthropic.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Falcon is built by <a href={url('/')}>{NAME}</a>. Questions or a miss Falcon didn’t catch?
      Email <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}?subject=Falcon`}>{EMAIL}</a> or open an issue on
      <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .cta {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
  }
  .button {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.6rem 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
    border-radius: 6px;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .button:hover {
    background: var(--text);
    color: var(--bg);
  }
  .video {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #000;
  }
  .video iframe {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    border: 0;
  }
  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
</style>
